# Documentation required

Every exported or non-exported **function**, **method**, and **class** must have a short human-readable doc comment describing what it does.

## Requirements

- Prefer JSDoc (`/** ... */`) on TypeScript/JavaScript.
- Describe **behavior and purpose**, not implementation trivia.
- Document non-obvious parameters, return values, and thrown errors.
- One or two sentences is enough when the name is clear; expand when behavior is subtle.
- Keep docs accurate when changing code — update the comment in the same change.

## Applies to

- Public and private/internal helpers alike
- Async functions, constructors, and class methods
- New code and any existing symbol you meaningfully edit

## Does not require

- Trivial one-liner property accessors that only return a field (optional)
- Test files may use brief docs; still document non-obvious helpers

## Examples

```typescript
// ❌ BAD — no description
export async function runReport(options: ReportOptions): Promise<StageReport> {
  // ...
}

// ✅ GOOD
/**
 * Regenerates the upgrade report for an existing `.beefup/staged` proposal.
 * Writes markdown and JSON under `.beefup/report`.
 */
export async function runReport(options: ReportOptions): Promise<StageReport> {
  // ...
}

// ❌ BAD — restates the name only
/** Diff resolutions. */
export function diffResolutions(a: Resolution, b: Resolution): ResolutionDiff {}

// ✅ GOOD — says what the result means
/**
 * Compares two lockfile resolutions and returns added, removed, upgraded,
 * and downgraded package changes for dependencies and devDependencies.
 */
export function diffResolutions(a: Resolution, b: Resolution): ResolutionDiff {}
```
